5 or something for Dots.\n\nBut Dots only have B6, so not.\n\nPerhaps the best is to discard a honor.\n\nLet's look at the answer choices.\n\nValid actions: Play F4, Play T1, Play T7, Play W7, Play T9, Play W5, Play J1, Play T3, Play T8, Play B6, Play F1, Play W6\n\nI should avoid discarding T1, T3, T9, T8, W5, W6, W7, B6 because they are part of sequences or pairs.\n\nDiscarding T7 is acceptable since I have two.\n\nDiscarding honors is good.\n\nSo candidates: Play F4, Play J1, Play F1, Play T7\n\nAmong these, Play T7 might be less safe because T7 is a middle tile, could be useful for chows, so discarding it might be claimed by others, but at early stage, low risk.\n\nPlay F4 or Play J1 might be safer.\n\nSince previous player played J1, Play J1 might be very safe.\n\nSimilarly, F4 is North, safe.\n\nBut for my hand, Play J1 removes a dragon, which might be good if I don't want honors.\n\nPlay F4 removes a wind.\n\nNow, F1 is East Wind, which is the first wind, sometimes used, but not for me.\n\nF4 is North, least used.\n\nSo Play F4 might be the best discard.\n\nIn some strategies, discarding the least relevant wind is good.\n\nPrevalent wind is West (F3), seat wind is South (F2), so F1 and F4 are not relevant.\n\nF4 is North, often considered the \"dead\" wind.\n\nSo discarding F4 is a common move.\n\nSimilarly, J1 is dragon, but dragons are always relevant for patterns.\n\nBut with only one, not.\n\nSo I think Play F4 is a good choice.\n\nPlay J1 is also good.\n\nBut let's see the unshown tiles: J1:3, so three left, including mine, so if I discard J1, it's the second played, so two played, two left.\n\nSimilarly for F4, full.\n\nBut no difference.\n\nPerhaps for scoring, if I keep J1, I might get Dragon Pung later, but low probability.\n\nSimilarly for F4.\n\nSo for now, discard F4.\n\nThen I can draw a tile that might help.\n\nFor example, draw T2, then I have potential for Two Terminal Chows.\n\nOr draw B5, for Dots sequence.\n\nBut with B6,B6, hard.\n\nAnothe