s one T7, so no pair. Whereas keeping two allows potential pung.\n\nHmm, trade-off.\n\nIf we discard T7:\n\n- Can still form T789 chow (with one T7)\n\n- Frees a slot, replaces with new tile\n\n- Avoids being stuck with an extra T7 if we don't draw a third\n\nIf we keep two T7s:\n\n- Chance to form pung (3 points for All Pungs, etc.)\n\nBut requires drawing another T7, which is possible but not guaranteed.\n\nGiven that we also have a B6 pair, focusing on two different pungs might be a path.\n\nBut with only two pairs now, it's a long shot.\n\nAlternatively, prioritize completing chows.\n\nSince we have natural sequences in W567 and potential T789, going for All Chows (2 points) plus other patterns.\n\nAll Chows is only 2 points, so need more.\n\nIf we have three chows and a pair, and maybe some other patterns.\n\nFor example, if the three chows are in different suits, Mixed Triple Chow (8 points).\n\nW567 (Characters), T789 (Bamboo), and say B... but we don't have Dots sequence.\n\nAlternatively, not quite.\n\nBut if we have two chows in Bamboo: T123 and T789, plus W567, that could be Pure Straight if all in same suit, but they're not.\n\nWait, Pure Straight requires all three in same suit.\n\nNo.\n\nMixed Triple Chow needs same numbers across suits.\n\nNot matching.\n\nHmm.\n\nBack to T7 discard. Let's say we discard T7.\n\nPros:\n\n- Keeps one T7 for T789 chow\n\n- Reduces redundancy\n\n- Makes room for potentially better tile\n\nCons:\n\n- Loses chance for T7 pung\n\nBut given that we have other avenues (B6 pair, W567), maybe better to streamline the hand.\n\nOther candidates: Discard T3 or T1.\n\nT3 seems less useful.\n\nWait, if we discard T3, we still have T1 which could be part of 123.\n\nBut need T2.\n\nProbability of drawing T2 is uncertain.\n\nSimilarly, discarding T1 keeps T3, which is still stranded.\n\nPerhaps discarding T3 is better.\n\nAlternatively, honors.\n\nGiven all, I think discarding T7 (one of the two) is strategic because:\n\n- Maintains ability to form T789 chow\n\n- Removes duplicate tile that might not be useful\n\n- Gives more flexibility\n\nWhereas discarding a singleton terminal or honor doesn't recover redundancy.\n\nYes, this seems good.\n\nLet me verify:\n\nCurrent T tiles: T1, T3, T7, T7, T8, T9.\n\nAfter discarding one T7: T1, T3, T7, T8, T9.\n\nCan still form T7,8,9 chow using the remaining T7.\n\nAnd T1 and T3 could potentially form part of another chow if T2 comes.\n\nBetter balance.\n\nWhereas keeping two T7s clutters the hand.\n\nTherefore, Play T7 is a strong candidate.\n\nOther options like discarding honors are safe but don't improve hand structure as much.\n\nSo Answer: Play T7.", "output": "Evaluation: The hand has potential for multiple chows (W5-6-7 and T7-8-9) and a B6 pair. Holding two T7s creates redundancy; discarding one maintains the T7-8-9 chow possibility while freeing space for more flexible draws. Honors (F1, F4, J1) and terminals (T1, T3, T9) are less critical to core sequences, but discarding a duplicate T7 optimizes hand efficiency over shedding singletons. \n\nReason: Keeping one T7 preserves the T7-8-9 chow opportunity, while reducing duplicates improves adaptability. Discarding T7 avoids cluttering the hand with excess T7s (only one needed for the chow), prioritizing structural cohesion over speculative pung development. This balances immediate sequence viability with future draw potential, aligning better with scoring patterns like Mixed Triple Chow (8 points) or All Chows (2 points + synergies).\n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
